{{/*
  shell — the whole page.

  The region containers below are the contract three things agree on: the
  partial envelope swaps into them by id (internal/app/partials.go), the
  carried stylesheet system files itself under their classes, and the seam
  reads the viewport's state node out of them. Each region's own template
  renders its own container, so a swap and a first paint produce the same
  bytes.

  Inheritance is declared once, here, and explicitly (issue #5 §4.3): every
  interaction on this page is about the same volume, so hx-vals says so at the
  top rather than every control repeating it, and nothing inherits by accident
  because nothing inherits without ":inherited" spelled out.
*/}}
{{define "shell"}}<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<meta name="color-scheme" content="dark">
<link rel="icon" href="data:,">
<title>{{.Title}} — Atlas</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/app.css">
<script src="/assets/htmx.js" defer></script>
</head>
<body class="atlas">
{{template "shell-region" .}}
{{template "import" .}}
{{/*
  The seam. It is loaded from /static, which is whatever tree the host mounted
  and answers 404 when it mounted none. An undefined <atlas-viewport> renders
  nothing and breaks nothing, which is the deletability principle costing this
  page one script tag (issue #5 §5.5).
*/}}
<script type="module" src="/static/app.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
{{end}}

{{/*
  shell-region — the page frame as a region.

  The document above is what a first paint answers with; this is what a swap
  answers with, and they are the same bytes because the document renders this.
  It matters that they are separable: the sidebar's collapsed state is a class
  on this element, and a swap can only set an attribute on a container it
  renders *itself* -- so the shell is an outer morph over this, and the
  document is never a partial.
*/}}
{{/*
  kbd-editable — the editable-target guard, spelled once and pasted into every
  keyboard filter that needs it (see the shortcuts block below).

  It is the reference implementation's isEditableTarget written as an
  expression, and `event.target instanceof Element` is doing real work at both
  ends. A select counts as editable: the reference says so, and a select
  answers the space bar itself. And a synthetic key dispatched straight at the
  window -- which is how a headless driver presses one -- has the window as
  its target, which is not an Element, so it passes as a keystroke nobody was
  typing.

  It carries no `]` and no comma on purpose: htmx reads a trigger's name up to
  the first `]`, and splits a trigger attribute on commas outside brackets, so
  either character would cut the filter in half.
*/}}
{{define "kbd-editable"}}!(event.target instanceof Element&&(/^(INPUT|TEXTAREA|SELECT)$/.test(event.target.tagName)||event.target.isContentEditable)){{end}}

{{define "shell-region"}}<div id="atlas-shell" class="app-shell{{if .Session.Sidebar.Collapsed}} sidebar-collapsed{{end}}"
     hx-vals:inherited='{"volume":"{{with .Volume}}{{.Slug}}{{end}}"}'
     hx-swap:inherited="none">
  <header id="atlas-topbar" class="topbar">{{template "topbar" .}}</header>
  {{template "legend" .}}
  <main class="map-panel">
    {{template "viewport-surface" .}}
    {{template "viewport" .}}
    <div class="zoom-controls" aria-label="Map controls">
      <button type="button" id="zoom-in" aria-label="Zoom in">+</button>
      <button type="button" id="zoom-out" aria-label="Zoom out">−</button>
    </div>
    {{template "grid-navigator" .}}
    <div class="map-corner">
      {{template "overview" .}}
      <div class="map-hint" id="map-hint">
        <span>Drag to pan</span><i></i>
        <span>Scroll to zoom</span><i></i>
        <span id="labels-hint">Z · hold for labels</span><i></i>
        <span id="grid-hint">{{if .Grid.Enabled}}G · {{.Grid.Name}}{{else}}G · grid off{{end}}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
  {{template "dock" .}}
  {{template "island" .}}
  {{/*
    The keyboard, as hypermedia.

    Every shortcut below moves discrete state, so every one of them is a route
    rather than a handler: an hx-trigger naming the key and the element the
    key is heard on, and nothing else. There is no hx-on here and no listener
    in the page. What is *not* here is the other half of the keyboard --
    holding Z, focusing a field, flipping panes, stepping the zoom, swallowing
    the webview's own menu -- because none of it moves discrete state and all
    of it belongs to the seam (issue #5 §4.1, render/keys.ts).

    They listen on the window rather than on the body, because that is where
    a keystroke with no focused control is heard: an event dispatched at the
    window never travels down to the body, so a shortcut listening there would
    hear a reader's own typing and nothing else.

    They are declared here rather than beside the controls they double for
    because a shortcut has to survive the region that would have carried it
    being swapped, and the shell is the one region a swap never replaces from
    underneath.

    THREE THINGS EVERY FILTER BELOW SAYS, and the page was broken for want of
    all three.

    It says whose keystroke it is. A shortcut heard on the window hears a
    reader typing into the search field too, so "g" put a grid up mid-word and
    the space bar in a hash flipped the subgrid; every filter but ⌘G carries
    "kbd-editable", which is the reference implementation's isEditableTarget
    written as an expression. ⌘G is deliberately above it, exactly as the
    reference has it: switching cell systems is most wanted from inside the
    token field.

    It says the key is answered. `prevent` is htmx's own preventDefault, and
    htmx runs it only when the filter passed -- so a shortcut suppresses the
    keystroke it acts on and nothing else, which is what stops macOS sounding
    the rejection tone at every press. ⌘R is the one place `prevent` takes
    something away and hands it straight back: the browser's own reload is
    swallowed, and the answer to the post is the same page fetched again --
    over a record that has just been thrown away.

    And it says the key was pressed rather than held. `!event.repeat` keeps an
    autorepeating key from posting sixty times a second down a route that
    writes a file.

    The filters read the live page where the state they need is not this
    template's to know -- whether the grid is up, whether the panel is folded.
    A value rendered into hx-vals here would be right once and stale for the
    rest of the session, because those regions swap and the shell does not.
  */}}
  <div hidden id="atlas-shortcuts">
    {{/*
      ⌘G cycles which cell system divides the map -- above the guard, so it
      answers from inside the token field.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"system":"cycle"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&key.toLowerCase()=='g'&&!event.repeat] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      ⌘R throws this volume's remembered state away and comes straight back to
      it: the refresh key doing what a reader already believes refreshing does,
      and the one way out of a record that has gone wrong -- five highlights
      culled at every pin, a filter nobody can find the row for -- without
      knowing which field is at fault.

      ABOVE THE GUARD, exactly where the reference had it: a reader whose page
      is misbehaving reaches for reload from wherever the cursor is, including
      a field they were typing in, and a reload that answers everywhere but
      there is a reload that has been half taken away.

      The answer is a redirect back into the volume (resetSession,
      internal/app/session.go) rather than a partial set: the record every
      region is rendered from is gone, so there is nothing to patch and the
      round trip is the refresh `prevent` just swallowed.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/reset"
          hx-trigger="keydown[(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&key.toLowerCase()=='r'&&!event.repeat] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      ⌘⌥B folds the panel beside the map, the shortcut editors give their
      secondary sidebar. BY PHYSICAL KEY: Option rewrites event.key on a Mac
      (⌥B types "∫"), so event.code is the one name the chord keeps
      everywhere -- and it is declared before ⌘B, whose filter refuses altKey,
      so the two chords can never both answer.

      TWO SPANS, not one, and that is the interesting part. The panel's own
      fold button knows which way it is going because the dock re-renders with
      it; a shortcut in the shell does not, because folding the dock swaps the
      dock alone. So the direction is not rendered at all: each span asks the
      button on screen which way it is pointing, and only the one that agrees
      answers. Nothing here can go stale, because nothing here is remembered.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/dock" hx-vals:append='{"open":"1","byHand":"1"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[event.code=='KeyB'&&(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&altKey&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}&&document.querySelector('#dock-fold')?.getAttribute('aria-expanded')=='false'] from:window prevent"></span>
    <span hx-post="/session/dock" hx-vals:append='{"open":"0","byHand":"1"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[event.code=='KeyB'&&(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&altKey&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}&&document.querySelector('#dock-fold')?.getAttribute('aria-expanded')=='true'] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      ⌘B puts the index away. This one *can* render its direction, because the
      sidebar's collapsed state is a class on the shell and the shell is what
      a sidebar swap re-renders -- so the value below is rewritten with it.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/sidebar"
          hx-vals:append='{"open":"{{if .Session.Sidebar.Collapsed}}1{{else}}0{{end}}"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[(metaKey||ctrlKey)&&!altKey&&key.toLowerCase()=='b'&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      G opens the grid and closes it. The reference hands the keyboard on as
      well -- the field that takes a hash is focused on the way in, the map on
      the way out, so "gm6" arrives at m6 -- and that half is focus rather
      than state, so it belongs to the seam and is noted as owed there
      (render/keys.ts).
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"system":"toggle"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key.toLowerCase()=='g'&&!metaKey&&!ctrlKey&&!altKey&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      Space divides the chosen cell, or stops dividing it -- only while there
      is a grid to divide, and never when the toggle itself is focused, which
      answers its own space bar and would otherwise be pressed twice.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"subgrid":"flip"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key==' '&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}&&document.querySelector('#atlas-grid-navigator')?.hidden===false&&event.target!==document.querySelector('#subgrid-toggle')] from:window prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      And the same key from inside the field, which the guard above shuts out
      by design: no hash will ever spell a space, and clearing the mesh is
      what is most wanted while typing one. It is heard on the field rather
      than the window, so it needs no guard of its own.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"subgrid":"flip"}'
          hx-trigger="keydown[key==' '&&!event.repeat] from:#grid-input prevent"></span>
    {{/*
      ESCAPE MEANS TWO THINGS AT ONCE, and both of them happen.

      Heard on the map it closes the card; heard on the window it telescopes
      the grid out one level, and out of the grid altogether once there is no
      address left. A press with the map focused is heard by both -- the first
      at its target, the second as it bubbles -- which is exactly what the
      reference did with one handler and two function calls.

      Two handlers means two requests, and hx-sync is what keeps that from
      being a race: both queue on the shell, so the card closes and *then* the
      grid ascends, and neither answer is computed from a record the other one
      has already moved on from.

      The third meaning is the field's, and it is a stopPropagation in the
      seam rather than a route: the first Escape in the grid input only hands
      the keyboard back to the map, and the second one gets here.
    */}}
    <span hx-post="/session/select" hx-vals:append='{"feature":""}'
          hx-sync="#atlas-shell:queue all"
          hx-trigger="keydown[key=='Escape'&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}] from:#map prevent"></span>
    <span hx-post="/session/grid" hx-vals:append='{"ascend":"1"}'
          hx-sync="#atlas-shell:queue all"
          hx-trigger="keydown[key=='Escape'&&!event.repeat&&{{template "kbd-editable"}}&&document.querySelector('#atlas-grid-navigator')?.hidden===false] from:window prevent"></span>
  </div>
  {{/*
    The camera's way home.

    The seam reports a settled camera so a volume reopens where the reader
    left it (issue #5 §4.1). It is the one upward flow that is not a reader's
    move, and it is *this page's* post rather than the seam's own: the
    application renders the form and the fields, the seam fills them in and
    raises the event, and htmx does the rest. That is the same arrangement the
    labels hint and the corner locator already stand in -- chrome the
    application renders for the seam to draw into (docs/render-seam.md §2) --
    and it is what lets the answer land. A report the seam posted for itself
    would throw the answer away, and the answer is the state island carrying
    the camera that was just written.
  */}}
  <form hidden id="atlas-camera" hx-post="/session/view"
        hx-trigger="atlas:camera from:window">
    <input type="hidden" name="world" id="atlas-camera-world"
           value="{{with .Volume}}{{.World.Slug}}{{end}}">
    <input type="hidden" name="x" id="atlas-camera-x">
    <input type="hidden" name="y" id="atlas-camera-y">
    <input type="hidden" name="zoom" id="atlas-camera-zoom">
    <input type="hidden" name="rotation" id="atlas-camera-rotation" value="0">
  </form>
  {{/*
    The pick's way in.

    The panes resolve a hit themselves -- a pixel is theirs and an identity is
    this application's (docs/render-seam.md §5) -- and this is the half that
    was missing: the seam handed an identity up and nothing was standing there
    to take it, so a click on the canvas moved nothing at all.

    It is the camera's arrangement exactly, and for the same reason. The
    application renders the form and the field it wants filled, the seam fills
    it in and raises the event, htmx posts it and applies the answer -- and the
    answer is the selection the legend marks and the dock and the card draw,
    which are the page's regions, not the seam's. A request the seam made for
    itself would throw all of that away.

    ONE FIELD, and the two absences are the point. The volume is not here
    because it is inherited from the shell above like every other interaction's
    on this page, and `focus` is not here because a pick off the canvas is not
    a jump: a row reached for from a list marks the ground it opened in the
    index, and a reader who clicked the ground was already standing on it
    (applySelect, internal/app/session.go).
  */}}
  <form hidden id="atlas-pick" hx-post="/session/select"
        hx-trigger="atlas:pick from:window">
    <input type="hidden" name="feature" id="atlas-pick-feature">
  </form>
  {{/*
    The cell's way in, which is the pick's way in for the other thing a
    surface can be pointed at.

    A reader telescopes through the grid three ways and they are one
    interaction: typing an address into the navigator's field, clicking a cell
    the chart drew, and pressing a point on the sphere -- which names the cell
    one level below the one being held. The field is a control the application
    already renders and posts for itself; the two surfaces are pixels, and a
    pixel is the seam's to resolve (docs/render-seam.md §5). So they arrive
    here, at the same route the field posts to and with the same field name,
    and the server normalizes and validates the address once for all three.

    IT POSTS `cell` AND NOTHING ELSE. Which system is dividing the map is
    already in the session -- the address was resolved *by* that system, and a
    seam telling the server which one it used would be the seam holding an
    opinion about state it does not own. The volume is inherited from the
    shell, as everything on this page is.
  */}}
  <form hidden id="atlas-grid-pick" hx-post="/session/grid"
        hx-trigger="atlas:grid-pick from:window">
    <input type="hidden" name="cell" id="atlas-grid-pick-cell">
  </form>
  {{/*
    Live updates. The stream carries ready-to-swap partials and two event
    names (docs/app.md §5); the page takes the plainer road of re-fetching
    itself on either, because both events mean the library moved under it and
    a page that re-reads its own URL cannot end up half-patched. The bodies
    stay in the contract for a client that wants the cheaper path.

    These stand inside #atlas-shell for the same reason the island does: the
    address they re-fetch is the address of the volume they were rendered
    for, and a pair left outside the shell goes on naming the volume the page
    first opened on. A reconcile would then take the reader somewhere else,
    which is exactly what an unchanged catalog must never do.
  */}}
  {{with .Volume}}<div hidden id="atlas-events" hx-sse:connect="/events?volume={{.Slug}}" hx-trigger="load"></div>
  <div hidden hx-get="/v/{{.Slug}}/{{.World.Slug}}"
       hx-trigger="catalog from:#atlas-events, refresh from:#atlas-events"
       hx-target="#atlas-shell" hx-select="#atlas-shell" hx-swap="outerMorph"></div>{{end}}
</div>
{{end}}
